WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.790
<v Instructor>So now let's talk about EC2 spot instances</v>

2
00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:04.890
and they allow you to get discounts

3
00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.080
up to 90% compared to on-demands.

4
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.040
How does it work?

5
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.530
Well, you define a max spot price

6
00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.600
and you're going to get the EC2 instance

7
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.460
as long as the current spot price,

8
00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.800
and it always determines that spot price

9
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.300
based on offer and demand.

10
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.190
So as long as the current spot price is less than your max,

11
00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:22.170
then you're going to get the instance.

12
00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:25.710
But then if the offer and the demand vary

13
00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.320
and the capacity varies, then the spot price may go up.

14
00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.320
And if the spot price goes over your max price,

15
00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.330
then you can choose to either stop

16
00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:35.310
or terminate your spot instance

17
00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.680
and you have a two minutes grace period.

18
00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.590
So this is where you can clearly shut down your application,

19
00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:42.630
save your files, and so on.

20
00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:46.770
So spot instances are great for batch jobs, data analysis

21
00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:50.160
or any kind of workload that is resilient to failure,

22
00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.830
but you should not use them for critical jobs

23
00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.320
or databases while because they can be reclaimed

24
00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.290
by AWS very quickly and you can lose them.

25
00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:01.470
So in terms of the spot price, what does it look like?

26
00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.660
So this is an example for M4 large

27
00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:07.440
for a duration of three months in US East 1.

28
00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.040
And as we can see it's the price varies based on the AZ.

29
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.500
So if you have one A, one B, one C, et cetera,

30
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.740
you have a different price and the price can vary over time.

31
00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.950
So if you define your user max price at here,

32
00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:23.640
this dotted black line that's horizontal,

33
00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.060
then as long as the price is underneath that black line,

34
00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:28.203
we get an instance,

35
00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:32.310
and then as long as it's above it, we lose, we terminate

36
00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:34.410
or stop that instance.

37
00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:38.010
And so as we can see, the spot price is quite lower

38
00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:39.570
than the on-demand price.

39
00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:42.660
So if your user defined max price, it's very high,

40
00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:44.310
then you will never lose your spot instance

41
00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:45.600
based on this graph.

42
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.000
But if it's quite low, for example, where it is right now,

43
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.920
there's a chance for example for the red line

44
00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:51.720
that sometimes the price is gonna be higher

45
00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.880
than what you want and sometimes it's gonna be lower

46
00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:54.713
than what you need.

47
00:01:54.713 --> 00:01:56.820
So it depends how you want to bid.

48
00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:59.010
But the idea is that with the spot instances,

49
00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:02.790
you want to define a max price that you're comfortable with

50
00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:04.860
for your economics, but at the same time

51
00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:07.830
that we'll make sure that if you lose your spot instance

52
00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.140
then you haven't lost your data and so on.

53
00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:11.970
Okay, next spot fleets.

54
00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.640
So this is the ultimate way to save money.

55
00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.280
So spot fleets is going to be a way to define

56
00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.400
for you to get a set of spot instances

57
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.250
and optionally on-demand instances,

58
00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:24.480
that's what it's called a fleet.

59
00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.210
And the spot fleet will try its best

60
00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:28.770
to meet the target capacity

61
00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:30.930
with the price constraints you're defined.

62
00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:34.170
So it will launch from possible launch pools.

63
00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:37.230
So it can have different instance types, different OS

64
00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:38.790
and availability zones.

65
00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:40.850
And so you're going to define multiple launch pools.

66
00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:43.050
So multiple instance types, multiple everything.

67
00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:46.530
And then the fleet will choose the best

68
00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.770
and most appropriate launch pool for you.

69
00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:53.340
And when your spot fleet either reaches your budget

70
00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.320
or reaches the capacity you wanted,

71
00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.990
then it will stop launching instances.

72
00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:00.210
And so you define a strategy

73
00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:02.760
to allocate spot instances in your spot fleet,

74
00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.200
and that's something to remember.

75
00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.360
The first one is lowest price,

76
00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.190
and that's gonna be the most popular one

77
00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:09.420
going into the exam,

78
00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:13.290
which is that the spot fleet will launch instances

79
00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:16.170
from the pool that has the lowest price

80
00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.540
and that gives you a lot of cost optimization.

81
00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:19.920
And this is a great option

82
00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.110
if you have a very short workload.

83
00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:24.360
You can also have a diversified way

84
00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:26.100
to launch your spot instances,

85
00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.730
in which case the launch in the spot instances

86
00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:32.010
will be distributed across all the pools

87
00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:33.570
that you have defined from before,

88
00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:36.000
which is great for availability and long workloads

89
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.770
because if one pool goes away,

90
00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:39.870
then your other pools are still active.

91
00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:41.370
Then you have capacity optimized.

92
00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:43.680
So you have a pool with the optimal capacity

93
00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.810
for the number of instances you want.

94
00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:47.940
And then you have price capacity optimized,

95
00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.260
which is first choosing the pool

96
00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:51.390
with the highest capacity available

97
00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:52.950
and then select within that

98
00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:54.450
the one that has the lowest price,

99
00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:57.480
which is the best choice for most workloads.

100
00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:00.210
So the idea is that spot fleets can be complicated,

101
00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:03.210
but what you have to remember is that using the spot fleet,

102
00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.220
you're able to define multiple launch pools,

103
00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:06.180
multiple instance types,

104
00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.280
and so you're just interesting in raw power.

105
00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:11.220
And then if you use the lowest price discounts

106
00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:13.770
at the lowest price strategy strategy for spot fleets,

107
00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:16.350
then the spot fleets will automatically request

108
00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:19.500
the spot instances with the lowest price for us.

109
00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:22.830
And so spot fleets gives us an extra saving

110
00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:24.270
based on spot instances

111
00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:25.950
because it's smart enough to choose

112
00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:28.200
the right spot instance pool to allow us

113
00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.330
to get the maximum amount of savings.

114
00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:33.090
And this is the benefits of spot fleets.

115
00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:35.040
And again, you need to understand the difference here

116
00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:38.400
of doing a very simple spot instance request

117
00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.470
where you know exactly the type of instance you want

118
00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.840
and the AZ you want to using a spot fleet

119
00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:45.120
and saying, okay, you can choose all these instance types

120
00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:47.610
and all these AZ, and what I need from you

121
00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:50.730
is to give me the lowest price, for example, okay?

122
00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.460
So that's hopefully is giving you a lot of insights

123
00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:56.160
into how spot fleets and spot instances are different.

124
00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:57.180
I hope you like this lecture

125
00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:59.130
and I will see you in the next lecture.

